1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,096
Why do traffic jams appear out of nowhere?

2
00:00:03,596 --> 00:00:06,337
You crawl for ten minutes, bumper to bumper.

3
00:00:07,837 --> 00:00:11,622
Then the road opens up, and there is nothing there.

4
00:00:11,622 --> 00:00:12,290
No crash.

5
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:13,255
No roadworks.

6
00:00:13,255 --> 00:00:14,369
No lane closed.

7
00:00:14,869 --> 00:00:16,131
So what stopped you?

8
00:00:16,631 --> 00:00:19,333
Most of us blame something up ahead.

9
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:20,383
A slow driver.

10
00:00:20,383 --> 00:00:21,584
Someone merging.

11
00:00:21,584 --> 00:00:23,535
A crash on the other side.

12
00:00:25,035 --> 00:00:26,939
Sometimes that is true.

13
00:00:26,939 --> 00:00:29,504
Real bottlenecks do cause jams.

14
00:00:29,504 --> 00:00:30,745
But not always.

15
00:00:31,245 --> 00:00:36,140
In 2008, a team of Japanese physicists set out to find out.

16
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:40,122
They built a road with no possible cause at all.

17
00:00:41,622 --> 00:00:43,555
And the jam showed up anyway.

18
00:00:45,355 --> 00:00:46,727
Here is their road.

19
00:00:46,727 --> 00:00:52,648
A circle, 230 metres around, marked out on a flat, empty track.

20
00:00:53,148 --> 00:00:55,517
22 cars, spaced evenly.

21
00:00:55,517 --> 00:01:02,775
Every driver was asked to do the same thing: drive steadily at about 30 km/h.

22
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:04,679
No traffic lights.

23
00:01:04,679 --> 00:01:05,693
No junctions.

24
00:01:05,693 --> 00:01:06,784
No slow lorry.

25
00:01:06,784 --> 00:01:11,774
Every driver can see the car in front, and nothing else matters.

26
00:01:13,274 --> 00:01:16,459
If any road should flow forever, it is this one.

27
00:01:16,959 --> 00:01:18,596
For a little while, it does.

28
00:01:19,096 --> 00:01:21,725
Then, within a few minutes, a knot appears.

29
00:01:23,225 --> 00:01:28,847
A cluster of cars slows, stops, and waits, while the rest of the ring keeps moving.

30
00:01:30,647 --> 00:01:32,613
Watch the knot, not the cars.

31
00:01:33,113 --> 00:01:34,960
The cars keep going forward.

32
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:40,239
They drive into the jam at the back, crawl through it, and leave from the front.

33
00:01:41,739 --> 00:01:46,473
But the jam itself drifts the other way, backwards, against the traffic.

34
00:01:46,973 --> 00:01:47,983
Why backwards?

35
00:01:47,983 --> 00:01:49,282
Look at the front.

36
00:01:49,282 --> 00:01:55,129
A car can only leave once the car ahead has pulled away, and that takes a moment.

37
00:01:56,629 --> 00:01:59,743
Meanwhile, new cars keep arriving at the back.

38
00:01:59,743 --> 00:02:05,700
So the front is eaten away, the back keeps growing, and the whole knot slides backwards.

39
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:10,999
In the experiment it moved backwards at roughly 20 km/h.

40
00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:17,633
That is close to the speed measured for stop-and-go waves on real motorways.

41
00:02:18,133 --> 00:02:19,775
So a jam is not a thing.

42
00:02:19,775 --> 00:02:25,109
It is a wave, passing through the cars, the way a ripple passes through water.

43
00:02:26,609 --> 00:02:28,955
And a wave needs something to start it.

44
00:02:30,755 --> 00:02:32,549
Zoom in on one driver.

45
00:02:33,049 --> 00:02:34,755
Nobody drives perfectly.

46
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:38,094
The gap ahead grows a little, shrinks a little.

47
00:02:38,094 --> 00:02:40,438
Your foot eases off for a moment.

48
00:02:40,938 --> 00:02:43,491
The driver behind you notices late.

49
00:02:43,491 --> 00:02:49,180
Human reactions take around a second, and when a surprise comes, often longer.

50
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:55,539
By the time they react, the gap has already closed more than they expected.

51
00:02:56,039 --> 00:02:59,808
So they brake a little harder than you did, just to be safe.

52
00:03:00,308 --> 00:03:02,614
Then the next driver brakes harder still.

53
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:09,049
Each car passes the slowdown back, and each one makes it slightly bigger.

54
00:03:09,549 --> 00:03:12,336
Traffic engineers have a name for this.

55
00:03:12,336 --> 00:03:17,909
A queue where each follower amplifies the one ahead is called string unstable.

56
00:03:18,409 --> 00:03:22,949
Far enough back, a gentle touch of the brakes can become a full stop.

57
00:03:25,049 --> 00:03:30,060
If you have sat in traffic in a big city, you have been one of the links in that chain.

58
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,985
Nobody in it did anything wrong.

59
00:03:32,985 --> 00:03:35,865
Every driver made a reasonable choice.

60
00:03:35,865 --> 00:03:39,503
The jam came from all of those choices together.

61
00:03:41,603 --> 00:03:42,281
But wait.

62
00:03:42,281 --> 00:03:47,104
If small wobbles always grow, why does traffic ever flow at all?

63
00:03:47,604 --> 00:03:50,882
Because they only grow when the cars are close together.

64
00:03:51,382 --> 00:03:55,586
With long gaps, a driver has room to absorb a slowdown.

65
00:03:55,586 --> 00:04:01,241
They ease off gently, and the wobble fades before it reaches the next car.

66
00:04:02,741 --> 00:04:05,042
With short gaps, there is no room.

67
00:04:05,042 --> 00:04:08,901
Every wobble has to be passed on, and passed on stronger.

68
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:12,121
Physicists call this an instability.

69
00:04:12,121 --> 00:04:15,671
Below a certain density, smooth flow is stable.

70
00:04:15,671 --> 00:04:18,240
Above it, smooth flow cannot last.

71
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:26,902
Motorway data in their paper put that threshold at around 25 cars per kilometre, in each lane.

72
00:04:27,402 --> 00:04:30,425
That is about one car every 40 metres.

73
00:04:30,425 --> 00:04:33,594
Busy roads squeeze below that all the time.

74
00:04:34,094 --> 00:04:38,270
Past that point, traffic does not need an accident to jam.

75
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:39,638
It only needs time.

76
00:04:41,738 --> 00:04:47,108
So if the jam comes from how we drive, can different driving make it go away?

77
00:04:47,608 --> 00:04:52,783
Later, engineers in Arizona repeated the ring with about 20 cars.

78
00:04:52,783 --> 00:04:55,484
The waves came back, just as before.

79
00:04:55,984 --> 00:04:58,457
Then they handed one car to a computer.

80
00:04:59,957 --> 00:05:01,403
Its rule was simple.

81
00:05:01,403 --> 00:05:03,500
Don't chase the car in front.

82
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:09,285
Hold a steady speed close to the average, and let the gap ahead grow and shrink.

83
00:05:09,785 --> 00:05:12,958
That one car soaked up each wave like a sponge.

84
00:05:13,458 --> 00:05:18,806
In their best run, the swings in speed dropped by about 80%.

85
00:05:18,806 --> 00:05:20,960
Fuel use fell by about 40%.

86
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:27,995
Even the number of cars getting round the ring went up, by as much as 14%.

87
00:05:28,495 --> 00:05:32,089
One calm car out of 20 changed the whole ring.

88
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:35,822
You can do a small version of this yourself.

89
00:05:35,822 --> 00:05:37,219
Leave a longer gap.

90
00:05:37,219 --> 00:05:40,084
Ease off early instead of braking late.

91
00:05:40,084 --> 00:05:43,905
Keep your speed steady instead of closing every gap.

92
00:05:43,905 --> 00:05:49,710
On a busy road others may cut in, so the effect is smaller, but it still helps.

93
00:05:51,210 --> 00:05:53,114
You will not get home sooner.

94
00:05:53,114 --> 00:05:55,281
But the drivers behind you might.

95
00:05:57,081 --> 00:06:00,019
So, why do traffic jams appear out of nowhere?

96
00:06:00,519 --> 00:06:03,142
Because they are not things in a place.

97
00:06:03,142 --> 00:06:08,659
They are waves, made by many drivers reacting a little late and a little too hard.

98
00:06:09,159 --> 00:06:14,501
Once the road is crowded enough, any small wobble can grow into a full stop.

99
00:06:14,501 --> 00:06:18,156
And that stop travels backwards through the traffic.

100
00:06:18,656 --> 00:06:24,315
And when you finally reach the front and the road opens up, the jam is still there.

101
00:06:24,315 --> 00:06:27,519
It has just moved on to the drivers behind you.

102
00:06:29,019 --> 00:06:32,010
Have you ever been stuck in a jam with no cause?

103
00:06:32,010 --> 00:06:33,880
Tell me where in the comments.
